body:
Finding (host, verified): with the daemon settings (vcodecd.c:599-600 seq_hdr_mode=1, prependheader=1) the vendor library writes the frame at the buffer START (W 0x064 = buffer base) and afterwards VENC_PrependHeader (libvcodec_utility 0x228d0) does on the CPU: memmove(bs_va+hdr_len, bs_va, frame_bytes) (call at 0x2295c), memcpy VPS/SPS/PPS to bs_va (0x22970-0x2298c), VENC_FlushRangeForIon (0x229a8). No invalidate before the memmove. Our guest view is a MIRROR (n2vc_dma.c), so the memmove reads the stale mirror (zeros) and the flush pushes zeros over the slice the VENC wrote. Reproduced with IV runner + N2VHOST_JOINED=1 (my fakevenc/apply_hevc_patches.py): H.264 -> 115837 B = SPS+PPS then zeros (FAILED); HEVC -> VPS/SPS/PPS then zeros. Same code runs in vcodecd on the phone -> the first phone H.264 encode through the daemon would return headers + zeros/garbage even with perfect hardware.
FIX (verified on host, both codecs, joined and separate modes still validate): V40 vcu/n2vcu.c vcu_gce_flush_ioctl, right after rc = gce_run_list(...):
  if (q.codec_type == VCU_VENC && n2vc_dma_lookup_pa && n2vc_dma_lookup_handle) {
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < cmd_cnt; i++) {
      uint64_t addr = ld64(c, cmds_addr + 0x800u + 8u * i);
      if (n2a32_ld8(c, cmds_addr + i) == 1 /*CMD_WRITE*/ && (addr & 0xfffu) == 0x064u) {   /* bitstream base, pa>>4 */
        uint32_t pa = (uint32_t)ld64(c, cmds_addr + 0x4800u + 8u * i) << 4, hd = 0, g0 = 0, rem = 0, pa0 = 0, tot = 0;
        if (n2vc_dma_lookup_pa(pa, &hd, NULL, &rem) == 0 && n2vc_dma_lookup_handle(hd, &pa0, &g0, &tot) == 0 && g0)
          (void)n2vc_dma_sync(&v->hw, v->mem, g0, pa - pa0, rem, 0);   /* device -> guest: DRAM is coherent after the job */
      } } }
(+ weak extern declarations of n2vc_dma_lookup_pa/_handle if n2vcu.c does not see them.) Semantics = what DRAM gives a CPU after a DMA write. Alternative workaround on IP side: seq_hdr_mode=0/prependheader=0, run one SEQ_HDR job at OPEN to fill g.s.hdr and let the existing shift_up() path insert it before each IDR (that path does device-side copies and is safe). With the V40 fix, H.264 joined still fails on the host only because opus-venc fake H.264 model cannot see the joined SPS/PPS (they are not in the buffer at trigger time) and uses Baseline defaults while the joined PPS differs (ffmpeg: deblocking params out of range) -> opus-venc: derive the config like I do for HEVC (vendor defaults + geometry) or learn the joined headers after the job; that is a fake-only problem, not a phone one.
